(* * * The free commutative monoid on a setoid, and its monad / * * 集上的自由交换幺半群及其幺半群子 *)

(*
 Book: Riehl, "Category Theory in Context", Example 5.1.5(ii), printed
 p. 185 (PDF p. 205) — riehl:5.1:example5, the appended box (b)
 of issue #471 (Mac Lane §VI.4, Exercise 1)
 nLab: https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/commutative+monoid
 nLab: https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/multiset
 nLab: https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/monadic+functor

 WHAT THE BOOK SAYS, read from the PDF.  "A modification of the free
 monoid monad yields the free commutative monoid monad defined by
 TA := ∐_{n≥0} (A^{×n})/S_n.  Elements are finite unordered lists of
 elements, or equally finitely supported functions χ : A → N."  The
 box asks for that monad on Sets, "lists modulo permutation, or
 finitely supported N-valued functions — justify the presentation in
 the header", as [Adjunction_Induced_Monad] of a FreeCMon ⊣
 CMon_Forget, with its Eilenberg–Moore category isomorphic to CMon.

 THE PRESENTATION, AND WHY.  Lists modulo permutation, the letters
 compared up to ≈: [perm_eq], generated by the empty list, a cons of
 ≈ letters, a swap of the first two letters and transitivity, and
 [BagObj X] the setoid of finite multisets it makes.  ∐_{n≥0}
 (A^{×n})/S_n is literally lists modulo permutations, and the
 presentation asks nothing of ≈.  Riehl's other reading, finitely
 supported χ : A → N, counts how often each a occurs, and counting
 occurrences up to ≈ needs ≈ decidable, which a setoid of Sets need not
 be: it is the presentation for sets with decidable equality, not for
 every object of Sets.  [perm_eq] is [Prop]-valued, so the PropEquiv
 field every CMon object carries since PR #1320 is free
 ([PropEquiv_of_relation] with identities); it stores Type-valued ≈
 premises and is eliminated only into the [Prop] mirror of a
 codomain's ≈ ([cfold_respects], the pattern of that PR's regime).
 Words, concatenation and the letterwise map are Instance/Mon/Free.v's
 [wapp] and [wmap], so no second list layer is written (a [bapp] would
 shadow Instance/Mon/Coproduct.v's); [wmap_wapp] and [wmap_id] are
 Leibniz equalities proved by induction, stronger than ≈.

 THE ADJUNCTION.  [FreeCMonObject X] is the multisets under
 concatenation with the empty one as zero ([FreeCMon_carrier],
 [FreeCMon_plus], [FreeCMon_zero] at [eq_refl]); [FreeCMon] maps the
 letters; [FreeCMon_adj : FreeCMon ⊣ CMon_Forget] is Theory/
 Adjunction.v's [Build_Adjunction'] from [FreeCMon_adj_iso] (forward
 g ↦ U g ∘ insert, backward the fold [cfold_hom], [cfold_unique] its
 uniqueness): the first left adjoint of [CMon_Forget] in the tree
 (Instance/Rng/AFT.v measured none, and a grep for a turnstile before
 [CMon_Forget] over the .v files found none before this file).

 THE MONAD.  [BagF] is U ◯ F and [BagMonad] its
 [Adjunction_Induced_Monad].  Its functor, unit and multiplication all
 compute ([Bag_obj]; [Bag_map], the letterwise map at a variable
 multiset; [Bag_ret]; [Bag_join_counit]; [Bag_join], the union
 [bconcat]), unlike W₀'s functor (Instance/Mon/Word.v), this free
 functor being built directly and not from universal arrows.

 ITS ALGEBRAS ARE THE COMMUTATIVE MONOIDS.  For an algebra α: a + b =
 α⟨a b⟩ ([balg_plus]), 0 = α⟨⟩ ([balg_zero]), commutativity by one
 swap ([balg_comm]), associativity and the unit from the algebra laws
 ([balg_assoc], [balg_zero_l]); α is the fold of that commutative
 monoid at ≈ ([balg_fold]).  Every algebra's carrier is a set:
 x ≈ y iff ⟨x⟩ and ⟨y⟩ are equal multisets ([balg_prop], #466's
 [talg_PropEquiv] pattern, read back at [eq_refl] by [balg_prop_is]),
 so the functor [EM_to_CMon] needs no hypothesis.  [CMon_K] is
 [EM_Comparison] of the adjunction ([CMon_K_alg_fun], [CMon_K_zero],
 [CMon_K_map] at [eq_refl]), [CMon_EM_iso : CMon ≅[Cat] BagAlg] has
 the legs [CMon_K] and [EM_to_CMon] ([CMon_EM_iso_to],
 [CMon_EM_iso_from]) and every component of both natural isomorphisms
 the identity at [eq_refl] ([CMon_EM_iso_to_from_component],
 [CMon_EM_iso_from_to_component]); an isomorphism in Cat is, by
 Instance/Cat.v, an equivalence of categories, and a strict one is
 refused.  [CMon_EM_equivalence] is the equivalence and
 [CMon_Forget_Monadic : Monadic CMon_Forget] the monadicity of
 commutative monoids over Set; [CMon_K_Forget] and [EM_to_CMon_Forget]
 commute with the forgetful functors.  The round trips keep the
 carrier, the zero and the maps at [eq_refl] ([CMon_rt_carrier],
 [CMon_rt_zero], [CMon_rt_hom], [EM_CMon_rt_carrier],
 [EM_CMon_rt_alg_hom]) and return the sum as a + (b + 0)
 ([CMon_rt_plus_fun]); refused at [eq_refl] (Test/ProbeWord471.v): the
 sum (R22), the whole commutative monoid (R23), the whole algebra (R24)
 and the composites against the identity functors (R25, R26).

 STRENGTHS.  Every [Example] holds at [eq_refl], and
 Test/ProbeWord471.v restates each one (C84 to C102 and C104 to
 C108).  Fifteen proofs end [Defined] (counted by token) and
 thirteen are load-bearing, measured by closing each alone [Qed] in a
 renamed copy of #471's five files and naming the first command that
 then stops: [FreeCMonObject] ([FreeCMon_carrier]), [bag_insert],
 [cfold_hom] and [FreeCMon] ([FreeCMon_adj_iso]), [FreeCMon_map]
 ([FreeCMon]), [FreeCMon_adj_iso] ([FreeCMon_adj]), [FreeCMon_adj]
 ([Bag_ret]), [balg_prop] ([balg_prop_is]), [EM_cmon_map]
 ([EM_to_CMon]), [EM_to_CMon] ([CMon_EM_counit_iso]),
 [CMon_EM_counit_iso] and [CMon_EM_unit_iso] ([CMon_EM_equivalence])
 and [CMon_EM_iso] ([CMon_EM_iso_to]); [CMon_EM_equivalence] and
 [CMon_Forget_Monadic] are [Defined] by the data convention only
 (closed [Qed], nothing stops).  Twenty-one lemmas end [Qed].

 UNIVERSES, read off [About] (every name, by script).  [perm_eq], its
 constructors and two schemes (whose one level [About] names u), the
 multiset setoid and the list lemmas bind one level with caps of the
 standard library only.  The section [FreeCMon] is over Sets@{o so}
 and CMon@{so o}, CMon's object level at Sets's; its constants bind
 @{o so} with o < so and CMon's own Set < so (its hand pin,
 Instance/CMon.v: harmless, since Set ≤ o < so); the thirty-three that
 mention the composite U ◯ F add [Compose]'s internal level (o < it),
